%N A066311 Numbers whose distinct prime factors are consecutive.
%C A066311 If k is a term, any power of k is also a term. Also all primes are terms. - _Zak Seidov_, Jun 25 2015
%H A066311 Robert Israel, <a href="/A066311/b066311.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (1..1000 from Harry J. Smith)
%e A066311 35 is included because 35 = 5 * 7 and 5 and 7 are consecutive primes.
%p A066311 select((numtheory:-pi @ max - numtheory:-pi @ min - nops) @ numtheory:-factorset = -1, [$2..1000]); # _Robert Israel_, Jun 25 2015
%t A066311 fi[n_]:=FactorInteger[n];Select[Range[2,5903],PrimeQ[#]||Length[fi[#]] < 2 ||Union[Differences[PrimePi[#[[1]]&/@fi[#]]]]=={1}&]
%t A066311 (* For first 1000 terms. - _Zak Seidov_, Jun 25 2015 *)
%o A066311 (PARI) isok(k) = { my(f=factor(k)[,1]); #f > 0 && primepi(f[#f])-primepi(f[1])==#f - 1 } \\ _Harry J. Smith_, Feb 10 2010
%Y A066311 Essentially the same as A073491.
%Y A066311 Cf. A066312 (a subsequence).
%K A066311 nonn,changed
%O A066311 1,1
%A A066311 _Leroy Quet_, Jan 01 2002
%E A066311 Offset changed from 0 to 1 by _Harry J. Smith_, Feb 10 2010
